From 05f2ea7016b3bfc93ce16fa9172f90f79de3ef48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Gillinger <christian.gillinger@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 20:03:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] genesys: document the GL126 and the OpticFilm 135i

The chip list in the man page's title and description, an AUTHORS entry,
and the description file's status.

The .desc entry moves from :untested to :good and its comment is
rewritten: it described a skeleton with bring-up in progress, which is no
longer what this is. It now says what the scanner actually does -- one
frame of the strip per scan chosen by the frame option, magazine handling
through two further options, the five resolutions and the infrared pass
-- and states plainly that the testing covers a single unit, because only
one exists for this work.
---
 AUTHORS                       | 3 ++-
 doc/descriptions/genesys.desc | 4 ++--
 doc/sane-genesys.man          | 6 +++---
 3 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/AUTHORS b/AUTHORS
index 71d95ce..127e839 100644
--- a/AUTHORS
+++ b/AUTHORS
@@ -33,7 +33,8 @@ Backends:
  fujitsu:       Randolph Bentson, Frederik Ramm, Oliver Schirrmeister,
                 m. allan noah (*)
  genesys:       Henning Geinitz, Gerhard Jaeger (*), Stéphane Voltz,
-                Pierre Willenbrock, Povilas Kanapickas (*)
+                Pierre Willenbrock, Povilas Kanapickas (*),
+                Christian Gillinger (GL126 / OpticFilm 135i)
  gphoto2:       Peter Fales
  gt68xx:        Sergey Vlasov, Andreas Nowack, David Stevenson, and
                 Henning Geinitz
diff --git a/doc/descriptions/genesys.desc b/doc/descriptions/genesys.desc
index 83414f8..23146ef 100644
--- a/doc/descriptions/genesys.desc
+++ b/doc/descriptions/genesys.desc
@@ -20,8 +20,8 @@
 :model "OpticFilm 135i"
 :interface "USB"
 :usbid "0x07b3" "0x1436"
-:status :untested
-:comment "GL126; backend skeleton, hardware bring-up in progress"
+:status :good
+:comment "GL126. 35mm film scanner: one frame of the strip per scan, selected by the 'frame' option (1-6); the magazine is loaded and ejected with the 'load-film' and 'eject-film' options. 600/1200/2400/3600/7200 dpi and an infrared pass. Tested on a single unit."
 
 :model "OpticFilm 7200"
 :interface "USB"
